<p class="tsi-lede">Every other vehicle humans have built cheats. A car pushes on the road, a boat pushes on water, a plane pushes on air. Each of them borrows momentum from something that was already there, which is why a car can carry its own weight in passengers and still have room for luggage.</p>

<p>A rocket in vacuum has nothing to push against. The only mass available to push on is the mass it brought with it, so every increment of speed has to be bought by throwing part of the vehicle backwards and never getting it back. That single constraint, followed honestly to its conclusion, produces the most consequential equation in spaceflight.</p>

<div class="tsi-eq">
  &Delta;<em>v</em> = <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> ln <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num"><em>m</em><sub>0</sub></span><span class="den"><em>m</em><sub>f</sub></span></span>
  <span class="tsi-eq-lbl">The rocket equation</span>
</div>

<p><em>v</em><sub>e</sub> is the exhaust velocity, the speed at which propellant leaves the nozzle relative to the vehicle. <em>m</em><sub>0</sub> is the mass you start with, fully fuelled. <em>m</em><sub>f</sub> is what remains when the tanks run dry: structure, engines, and payload. Their ratio is the <strong>mass ratio</strong>, and it is the only thing about your vehicle's mass that the equation cares about. And &Delta;<em>v</em>, delta-v, is the total change in velocity the vehicle can produce.</p>

<p>Konstantin Tsiolkovsky derived it in 1897 and published it in 1903, working alone as a provincial schoolteacher in Kaluga. The same result had been obtained by William Moore in 1813 and would be found independently again by Robert Goddard and Hermann Oberth. It keeps being rediscovered because it is not really about rockets. It is about momentum conservation, and it was waiting for anyone who bothered to ask the question properly.</p>

<h2>Deriving it</h2>

<p>The derivation is short enough to do in your head once you have seen it, and it is worth doing once so the logarithm stops feeling arbitrary.</p>

<p>Work in an inertial frame and consider one instant. Before the instant, the vehicle has mass <em>m</em> and velocity <em>v</em>, so the system momentum is <em>mv</em>.</p>

<p>During the instant, the rocket expels a small parcel of propellant of mass d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub>. Two things now exist. The rocket, lighter by d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub> and faster by d<em>v</em>. And the parcel, moving at <em>v</em> &minus; <em>v</em><sub>e</sub>, because it was thrown backwards at <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> <em>relative to the rocket</em>, and the rocket was itself moving at <em>v</em>.</p>

<p>Nothing external acts on the system, so momentum is unchanged:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq plain">
  <em>mv</em> = (<em>m</em> &minus; d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub>)(<em>v</em> + d<em>v</em>) + d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub>(<em>v</em> &minus; <em>v</em><sub>e</sub>)
</div>

<p>Expand the right-hand side and almost everything cancels:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq plain">
  <em>mv</em> = <em>mv</em> + <em>m</em>&thinsp;d<em>v</em> &minus; <em>v</em>&thinsp;d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub> &minus; d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub>&thinsp;d<em>v</em> + <em>v</em>&thinsp;d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub> &minus; <em>v</em><sub>e</sub>&thinsp;d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub>
</div>

<p>The <em>mv</em> terms cancel, and both <em>v</em>&thinsp;d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub> terms cancel against each other. The term d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub>&thinsp;d<em>v</em> is a product of two infinitesimals, second order, and vanishes in the limit. What survives is:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq plain">
  <em>m</em>&thinsp;d<em>v</em> = <em>v</em><sub>e</sub>&thinsp;d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;&nbsp;
  d<em>v</em> = <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num">d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub></span><span class="den"><em>m</em></span></span>
</div>

<p>This intermediate line is the physically interesting one, and it deserves a moment. Each kilogram of propellant delivers the same fixed <em>momentum</em>, <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> per kilogram. But the <em>velocity</em> it buys is that momentum divided by whatever the vehicle currently masses. Since the vehicle is getting lighter as it burns, propellant spent late in the burn is worth more than propellant spent early.</p>

<p>Now switch from propellant expelled to vehicle mass. The vehicle loses exactly what the nozzle gains, so d<em>m</em><sub>p</sub> = &minus;d<em>m</em>, and integrating from the wet mass to the dry mass:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq plain">
  &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>&Delta;v</sup> d<em>v</em> = &minus;<em>v</em><sub>e</sub> &int;<sub><em>m</em><sub>0</sub></sub><sup><em>m</em><sub>f</sub></sup> <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num">d<em>m</em></span><span class="den"><em>m</em></span></span>
</div>

<p>The integral of d<em>m</em>&thinsp;/&thinsp;<em>m</em> is ln <em>m</em>. That is the whole answer to "where does the logarithm come from": it is what you get when the thing you are dividing by is the same thing you are consuming.</p>

<div class="tsi-eq">
  &Delta;<em>v</em> = &minus;<em>v</em><sub>e</sub>(ln <em>m</em><sub>f</sub> &minus; ln <em>m</em><sub>0</sub>) = <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> ln <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num"><em>m</em><sub>0</sub></span><span class="den"><em>m</em><sub>f</sub></span></span>
</div>

<h2>Why the logarithm is brutal</h2>

<p>Read forwards, the equation looks gentle. Burn more propellant, gain more speed, with diminishing returns. Nothing alarming.</p>

<p>The alarm arrives when you read it backwards. You do not usually get to choose your mass ratio and see what speed falls out. You have a mission, the mission demands a specific &Delta;<em>v</em>, and you have to find a vehicle that delivers it. Solve for mass ratio and the logarithm inverts:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq">
  <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num"><em>m</em><sub>0</sub></span><span class="den"><em>m</em><sub>f</sub></span></span> = <em>e</em><sup>&thinsp;&Delta;<em>v</em>&thinsp;/&thinsp;<em>v</em><sub>e</sub></sup>
  <span class="tsi-eq-lbl">The same equation, read as a requirement</span>
</div>

<p>Your mission requirement now sits in an exponent. With a realistic <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> of 3,000 m/s, buying 3 km/s costs 63% of your liftoff mass in propellant. Buying 6 km/s costs 86%. Buying 9 km/s, roughly what it takes to reach low Earth orbit, costs 95%, and every engine, tank, wire, weld and gram of payload has to fit inside the remaining 5%.</p>

<p>Drag the sliders below and watch the curve go vertical. The interesting region is the far right, where each additional percentage point of propellant fraction is bought at a savage price in structural margin.</p>

