<h2>Engines beat tanks, and it is not close</h2>

<p>Look again at where the two variables sit. <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> multiplies. Mass ratio passes through a logarithm first. That asymmetry is the entire design logic of rocketry, and it is worth making concrete.</p>

<p>Take a vehicle at 90% propellant fraction with <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> = 3,000 m/s. It has 6,908 m/s of &Delta;<em>v</em>. Now try to buy roughly 2,000 m/s more, two ways.</p>

<div class="tsi-scroller">
  <table class="tsi-table">
    <caption>Two routes to about 8,980 m/s</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Route</th><th class="num">Propellant</th><th class="num"><em>v</em><sub>e</sub></th><th class="num">&Delta;<em>v</em></th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Baseline</td><td class="num">90%</td><td class="num">3,000</td><td class="num">6,908</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Build lighter</td><td class="num">95%</td><td class="num">3,000</td><td class="num">8,987</td><td>Half your entire dry mass budget, gone</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Build better engines</td><td class="num">90%</td><td class="num">3,900</td><td class="num">8,980</td><td>A 30% better engine, airframe untouched</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p>Halving the dry mass budget is the difference between a buildable vehicle and an aluminium foil fantasy. A 30% improvement in exhaust velocity is a hard engineering problem, but it is a normal one. The logarithm punishes you for chasing mass fraction and rewards you generously for chasing exhaust velocity.</p>

<p>Which raises the obvious question: why not just keep improving the engine?</p>

<h3>Specific impulse, and the chemical ceiling</h3>

<p>Engineers usually quote exhaust velocity as <strong>specific impulse</strong>, <em>I</em><sub>sp</sub>, measured in seconds. The two are the same number in different clothes:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq plain">
  <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> = <em>I</em><sub>sp</sub> &middot; <em>g</em><sub>0</sub>, &nbsp; where <em>g</em><sub>0</sub> = 9.80665 m/s<sup>2</sup>
</div>

<p>The <em>g</em><sub>0</sub> here is a unit conversion, not a statement about local gravity. Specific impulse is the same in orbit as on the pad.</p>

<div class="tsi-scroller">
  <table class="tsi-table">
    <caption>Practical exhaust velocities, vacuum figures</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Propellant</th><th class="num"><em>I</em><sub>sp</sub> (s)</th><th class="num"><em>v</em><sub>e</sub> (m/s)</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Solid propellant</td><td class="num">~270</td><td class="num">~2,650</td><td>Simple, throttle-free, not restartable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kerosene + LOX</td><td class="num">310&ndash;340</td><td class="num">3,040&ndash;3,330</td><td>Dense, storable-ish, the workhorse</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Methane + LOX</td><td class="num">360&ndash;380</td><td class="num">3,530&ndash;3,730</td><td>Clean-burning, restartable, reuse-friendly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hydrogen + LOX</td><td class="num">440&ndash;465</td><td class="num">4,310&ndash;4,560</td><td>The chemical ceiling. Bulky, cryogenic, awkward</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<p>That last row is a wall, not a frontier. Exhaust velocity in a chemical rocket is set by combustion temperature and the molecular weight of the exhaust, and you want the hottest possible flame producing the lightest possible molecules. Hydrogen and oxygen are already close to the best the periodic table offers. No amount of money or cleverness gets a chemical engine to 8,000 m/s.</p>

<h2>Thrust is not efficiency</h2>

<p>Which invites a natural but wrong idea: use an ion thruster. They reach exhaust velocities around 30,000 m/s, nearly ten times the chemical ceiling. Why is anything still burning kerosene?</p>

<p>Because efficiency and thrust are different quantities, and in electric propulsion they actively trade against each other.</p>

<div class="tsi-eq plain">
  <em>F</em> = <em>&#7745;</em> <em>v</em><sub>e</sub>
</div>

<p>Thrust is mass flow rate times exhaust velocity. An ion thruster achieves its enormous <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> by accelerating an extraordinarily thin stream of xenon to extraordinary speed. The <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> term is huge, the flow rate term is minuscule, and the product is tiny.</p>

<p>It gets worse once power enters. The kinetic energy going into the exhaust is <em>P</em> = &frac12; <em>&#7745;</em> <em>v</em><sub>e</sub><sup>2</sup>, so for a fixed power supply, substituting for the flow rate gives:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq">
  <em>F</em> = <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num">2<em>P</em></span><span class="den"><em>v</em><sub>e</sub></span></span>
  <span class="tsi-eq-lbl">At fixed power, thrust falls as efficiency rises</span>
</div>

<p>Thrust is <em>inversely</em> proportional to exhaust velocity at fixed power. Every unit of efficiency is paid for directly in thrust. A chemical rocket escapes this trap because its energy arrives inside the propellant itself, as chemical bonds, so available power scales with flow rate instead of being capped by a solar array.</p>

<p>The magnitudes are almost comic. The NSTAR ion thruster that flew on the Dawn spacecraft produced about 92 millinewtons, roughly the weight of a coin resting in your palm. A single Merlin engine produces about 845,000 newtons. Ion propulsion is superb for spending years quietly accelerating in deep space, and completely useless for departing or arriving anywhere with meaningful gravity.</p>

<div class="tsi-callout">
  <span class="tag">The equation's blind spot</span>
  <p>The rocket equation contains no time, no thrust, and no gravity. It will cheerfully hand you a &Delta;<em>v</em> figure for a vehicle that cannot lift itself off the ground. Any simulation or design study needs a thrust-to-weight constraint sitting alongside it, because &Delta;<em>v</em> tells you what you can afford, not whether you can move.</p>
</div>

<h2>Staging: cheating the logarithm</h2>

<p>Suppose you want 9,400 m/s, the realistic cost of reaching low Earth orbit from the surface of the Earth, using kerosene at <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> = 3,000 m/s. The required mass ratio is <em>e</em><sup>3.13</sup> &asymp; 22.9, so your dry mass, everything that is not propellant, must be under 4.4% of liftoff mass. Tanks, engines, plumbing, avionics, structure and payload, all inside one part in twenty-three. That is beyond what chemical vehicles with useful payloads have achieved.</p>

<p>Now split the job across two stages, each delivering 4,700 m/s. Each needs a mass ratio of only <em>e</em><sup>1.57</sup> &asymp; 4.79, meaning a dry mass fraction of about 21%. That is comfortably buildable.</p>

<p>The trick is that <em>m</em><sub>f</sub> punishes you for every gram you are still carrying at burnout, and an empty first-stage tank is pure dead weight. Dropping it lets you restart the logarithm with a much smaller vehicle. Staging is not an optimisation, it is the reason orbital flight is possible at all.</p>

<h2>What the equation leaves out</h2>

<p>The clean derivation assumed an isolated system with no external forces. Reality supplies several, and they show up as extra &Delta;<em>v</em> you must budget for but never see as velocity.</p>

<p><strong>Gravity losses.</strong> While your engine burns, gravity keeps pulling. The velocity you actually gain is the thrust term minus a gravity term:</p>

<div class="tsi-eq plain">
  <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num">d<em>v</em></span><span class="den">d<em>t</em></span></span> = <span class="tsi-frac"><span class="num"><em>F</em></span><span class="den"><em>m</em></span></span> &minus; <em>g</em> sin&thinsp;&gamma;
</div>

<p>where &gamma; is the flight path angle. Ascend vertically for 100 seconds and you have handed roughly 981 m/s to gravity for nothing. A typical launch to orbit loses something like 1.5 km/s this way, plus 100 to 300 m/s to atmospheric drag. This is why orbital velocity at low Earth orbit is 7.8 km/s but the budget is 9.4 km/s, and why rockets pitch over so early: horizontal thrust does not fight gravity.</p>

<p><strong>Finite burn time.</strong> Escape velocity assumes a single instantaneous kick. Any real burn takes minutes, during which the vehicle is climbing out of the well the whole time, so the impulsive figure is a floor rather than an estimate.</p>

<p><strong>Varying exhaust velocity.</strong> Nozzles are tuned for a particular ambient pressure. A sea-level engine underperforms in vacuum and a vacuum nozzle cannot be used at sea level, so <em>v</em><sub>e</sub> drifts through ascent.</p>

<h3>The landing case</h3>

<p>Propulsive landing is where all of this bites hardest, because it is the regime in which the classical equation is least informative on its own.</p>

<p>Hovering is the clearest illustration. A hovering vehicle produces thrust exactly equal to its weight and makes no progress whatsoever. It is spending &Delta;<em>v</em> at a rate of <em>g</em> per second, about 9.81 m/s of budget every second on Earth, purchasing nothing. Gravity losses are total.</p>

<p>The efficient answer is counterintuitive and slightly alarming: fall as long as possible, then burn as hard as possible as late as possible, arriving at zero velocity exactly at zero altitude. Minimising burn duration minimises the time gravity spends taxing you. This is the constant-thrust landing burn, known in the trade as a suicide burn or hoverslam, and it is optimal precisely because the rocket equation says nothing about hovering while physics charges you for it.</p>

<p>Two consequences follow for anyone modelling a lander. High thrust-to-weight is not a luxury but the dominant term in landing efficiency. And the landing propellant has to be carried all the way up and all the way back, which means it appears in <em>m</em><sub>f</sub> for every earlier burn, sitting inside the logarithm and quietly taxing the entire mission.</p>

<p>The <a href="/lab/rocket-landing">Rocket Landing</a> lab is that argument made runnable: its booster carries 5,200 kg of propellant on a 22,200 kg dry mass, which is a mass ratio of 1.234 and about 582 m/s of &Delta;<em>v</em> for the whole descent. Watching the guidance law decide <em>when</em> to light the engine is watching it spend that budget.</p>

<h2>The equation in one paragraph</h2>

<blockquote>Speed is bought with mass, and the currency is exponential. Exhaust velocity multiplies your budget; mass ratio only ever enters through a logarithm. So better engines beat lighter tanks, chemistry caps the engines at roughly 4.5 km/s, staging exists to restart the logarithm partway up, and none of it accounts for gravity, which will charge you separately.</blockquote>

